  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Mandela is an 82-year-old man with unfavorable intermediate-risk prostate cancer, seven weeks after his first leuprolide injection, who is due to start radiotherapy in about six weeks. He returns for his subsequent annual wellness visit and completed the health risk assessment beforehand. He finished four weeks of bicalutamide without trouble. Since the injection he has had mild hot flashes, three or four a week, which wake him about once a week; his energy is a little lower in the afternoons, but he still walks every morning. His mood is good and he notices no change in memory or concentration. He has had no breast tenderness, chest pain, breathlessness, or leg swelling. Urinary symptoms are unchanged and mild. He takes vitamin D daily. He has had no falls, emergency visits, or hospital stays in the past year. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Subsequent annual wellness visit</td></tr><tr><td>Safety events</td><td>No falls, emergency visits, or hospital stays in the past year</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Leuprolide 22.5 mg every twelve weeks (second dose due in five weeks); cholecalciferol 1000 units daily; bicalutamide finished four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Active conditions</td><td>Prostate cancer, unfavorable intermediate risk, on androgen deprivation before radiotherapy; low bone mass at the femoral neck</td></tr><tr><td>Functional status</td><td>Independent in all activities and instrumental activities of daily living</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient before the visit and reviewed during the visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Good</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Busy with his foundation and visitors; close family and friends; no loneliness or low mood</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Never used tobacco</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol and drugs</td><td>No alcohol (AUDIT-C 0); no other drug use</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Walks 30 to 45 minutes most mornings; light resistance exercises twice a week added for bone health</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Regular balanced meals; dairy and leafy greens for calcium; weight up 0.6 kg since starting androgen deprivation</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Seven hours a night; hot flashes wake him about once a week</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent</td></tr><tr><td>Instrumental activities of daily living</td><td>Independent; his assistant manages his calendar and he manages his own medicines</td></tr><tr><td>Home safety</td><td>Smoke detectors working; handrails on the stairs; good lighting</td></tr><tr><td>Falls</td><td>No falls this year; no fear of falling</td></tr><tr><td>Hearing and vision</td><td>Some difficulty hearing in crowded rooms; wears glasses; eye examination five months ago</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>No concerns</td></tr><tr><td>Pain</td><td>Occasional knee stiffness in the morning</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

